// DfProfile.pkg - per-method timing for instrumented DataFlex source.
//
// Used only by an instrumented *copy* of a workspace's source; nothing here belongs in an
// application. The instrumenter inserts `Send DfProfEnter <id>` at the first executable statement
// of a procedure or function, and `Send DfProfExit <id>` before every return and before the
// closing keyword.
//
// The same two constraints as DfCoverage.pkg apply, for the same reasons:
//
//   * `DfProfEnter` and `DfProfExit` are *global* procedures, so a probe needs no object handle in
//     scope and the inserted line is a single `Send` wherever it lands.
//
//   * State lives in `Global_Variable` arrays, never array properties. DataFlex gets and sets an
//     array property whole, with copy-on-write, so a property would copy the entire array on every
//     call -- fatal for something invoked on every method entry.
//
// `Use UI` for the same reason too: a global procedure declared before the base runtime is loaded
// compiles silently and registers nothing, and every later `Send` then fails with
// "Forward reference not resolved".
//
// COST, measured rather than assumed. Over 300,000 calls of a method that does one addition:
//
//     bare                          ~0.5 s
//     probes, counters only         ~1.6 s      (~2 us per probe)
//     probes, with the clock        ~3.4 s      (~5 us per enter/exit pair)
//
// About half of that is the `Send` to a global procedure, which is simply what a DataFlex message
// costs; the rest is `CurrentDateTime`. A cheaper clock would therefore save at most half.
//
// What this means in practice: a method doing real work -- a database read, an HTTP call, building
// a page -- is measured to well within a percent. A trivial method called hundreds of thousands of
// times is not, and its own time will be swamped by the measurement. Profile a view or a package,
// not a whole workspace, and read a hot micro-method's number with suspicion.
//
// For scale: the existing coverage instrumentation probes every *basic block* and pays the same
// ~2 us per probe, so per-method profiling is the cheaper of the two.

Use UI

// Wall-clock milliseconds spent inside each method, keyed by probe id.
Global_Variable Number[] ganDfProfTotal
// How many times each method was entered.
Global_Variable Integer[] gaiDfProfCalls
// How many times each method was left. Reported, not corrected: a method whose counts differ was
// left by a path the instrumenter could not see, and its time is not to be trusted.
Global_Variable Integer[] gaiDfProfExits

// The call stack: probe id and entry time, one entry per active call.
Global_Variable Integer[] gaiDfProfStack
Global_Variable DateTime[] gadtDfProfStarted
Global_Variable Integer giDfProfDepth

/// Records entry to the method with this probe id.
Procedure DfProfEnter Global Integer iProbe
    // A DataFlex array grows on an indexed write but errors on an indexed read past its end, so a
    // counter has to exist before it can be incremented -- the same guard DfCoverage.pkg needs,
    // and for the same reason: without it the first probe to fire takes the run down through the
    // error trap before anything is written.
    If (iProbe < SizeOfArray(gaiDfProfCalls)) Begin
        Move (gaiDfProfCalls[iProbe] + 1) to gaiDfProfCalls[iProbe]
    End
    Else Begin
        Move 1 to gaiDfProfCalls[iProbe]
    End

    Move iProbe to gaiDfProfStack[giDfProfDepth]
    // The entry time is kept as a DateTime and subtracted at exit, so no epoch has to be invented.
    Move (CurrentDateTime()) to gadtDfProfStarted[giDfProfDepth]
    Move (giDfProfDepth + 1) to giDfProfDepth
End_Procedure

/// Records the matching exit, adding the elapsed time to that method's total.
Procedure DfProfExit Global Integer iProbe
    Number nElapsed
    Integer iAt

    If (giDfProfDepth < 1) Begin
        // Left a method that was never entered. Nothing sensible to record; do not underflow.
        Procedure_Return
    End

    Move (giDfProfDepth - 1) to iAt

    // The top of the stack should be this probe. When it is not, the program left some method by
    // a path with no exit probe -- an early return the instrumenter could not reach, or a longjmp
    // out of an error trap. Unwind to this probe rather than mis-attributing everything above it.
    While (iAt >= 0 and gaiDfProfStack[iAt] <> iProbe)
        Move (iAt - 1) to iAt
    Loop

    If (iAt < 0) Begin
        // This probe is not on the stack at all; leave the stack as it was.
        Procedure_Return
    End

    Move (SpanTotalMilliseconds(CurrentDateTime() - gadtDfProfStarted[iAt])) to nElapsed
    If (iProbe < SizeOfArray(ganDfProfTotal)) Begin
        Move (ganDfProfTotal[iProbe] + nElapsed) to ganDfProfTotal[iProbe]
    End
    Else Begin
        Move nElapsed to ganDfProfTotal[iProbe]
    End

    If (iProbe < SizeOfArray(gaiDfProfExits)) Begin
        Move (gaiDfProfExits[iProbe] + 1) to gaiDfProfExits[iProbe]
    End
    Else Begin
        Move 1 to gaiDfProfExits[iProbe]
    End

    Move iAt to giDfProfDepth
End_Procedure
